Well, I must agree that the origins migbt have seemed little complex,=20
and perhaps even obscure, see below. The last part of the sentence=20
traces the origins of *Rogue* correctly AFAIK.

To illustrate the complexity of the Nethack origins in 1988, I'll quote=20
from the credits section of the version "2.0" Guide Book (which was=20
included in the December 1987 2.2a and the May 1988 2.3e releases):
---- begin quote (some minor typos corrected) ----
The original hack game was modeled on the Berkeley UNIX 'rogue' game. =20
Large portions of this paper were shamelessly cribbed from A Guide to=20
the Dungeons of Doom, by Michael C. Toy and Kenneth C. R. C. Arnold.
NetHack is the product of literally dozens of people's work. A list of=20
some of those who made major additions to the game appears below:
Jay Fenlason wrote the original release of "Hack", with help from Kenny=20
Woodland, Mike Thome and Jon Payne.
Andries Brouwer did a major re-write on the program and published (at =20
least) two versions to the Usenet.
Don G. Kneller ported the 1.0.3 version of Hack to the PC, creating PC-
Hack.

The following folks didn't actually re-write the game, or port it to a=20
new machine, but have made significant contributions to the playability=20
of the game:
[more names and deeds]
---- end quote ----

Furthermore, the README file references additional information:
"For a detailed history of the game, see the README.ORIG file, which=20
contains three separate readme files, dating back to the original=20
release of the game (then named hack) by Jay Fenlason."

And, as promised, README.ORIG contains further credits info which I=20
won't be quoting here...=20


> - "original nethack commands were text sentences" - ???
Another most-probably-not-perfect re-translation from German by me:
"The origin of Nethack was a pure text game, wherein the individual game=20
sections consisted only of in- and output of text. With the worldwide=20
standardization of terminal standards by ANSI, Nethack received its=20
graphical presentation."

> Is this all true? (Don't think so.)

Nethack was based on Hack which was based on Rogue (as we all know).=20
Rogue creators were very heavily inspired by "[Colossal Cave]=20
Adventure" (aka "ADVENT"), which is (AFAIK) *the* first computer=20
adventure game and what the writer most probably refers to.

See what Glenn Wichman (Rogue coauthor) himself writes about Colossal=20
Cave Adventure as the origin of Rogue:
---- begin quote ----
At computer labs in universities, most students used 'dumb terminals'=20
[...]. These terminals had no graphics capabilities. Programs would just=20
output text, which would scroll off the screen and disappear.
A popular game on college computers at the time was a text-only role=20
playing game called 'Adventure'. The computer would print out a textual=20
description of your surroundings, and you would respond by typing in a=20
command telling what your character should do [...].
Around this time at U.C.Berkeley, a student named Ken Arnold [...] put=20
together a library of routines which allowed programs to do 'cursor=20
addressing', which means the programs could put a character at a=20
specific location on the computer screen. [...] Now you could use=20
letters, numbers, and symbols to simulate pictures. The library of=20
software routines was called 'curses'.
As this package began to make the rounds to other universities, it was=20
picked up by [...] Michael Toy & myself [...]. We both enjoyed playing=20
'Adventure' (Michael had long ago mastered the program; I kept getting=20
killed but enjoyed it anyway).
Being a creative pair, we immediately set about seeing what kind of fun=20
we could have with the new curses library. After fooling around with a=20
couple simple games, we considered whether we could use this package to=20
do a 'graphical' adventure game. Once we decided it could be done,=20
things moved forward pretty quickly. [...] The name 'Rogue' was my idea.
---- end quote ----
- from http://www.wichman.org/roguehistory.html


